WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.375
Welcome to CSC-244.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:08.212
In this lesson, you’ll write reusable Kotlin functions, supply default and

3
00:00:08.212 --> 00:00:13.216
named arguments, and handle missing text with nullable types, safe calls, and

4
00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:14.050
a fallback.

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.350
A function gives a useful operation a name.

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.800
Null safety makes the possibility of missing data explicit.

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.745
These skills let you centralize app rules instead of repeating them in every

8
00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:28.125
screen.

9
00:00:28.375 --> 00:00:33.246
They also help you decide what users should see when information is

10
00:00:33.246 --> 00:00:36.900
unavailable, before a missing value becomes a runtime error.

11
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.375
Imagine you’re building the header for an Android notes app.

12
00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:45.175
The header needs a display name and a note-count label.

13
00:00:45.425 --> 00:00:48.100
A supplied name should appear in uppercase.

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.125
A missing name needs a readable fallback.

15
00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:55.974
Our sample calls use the name Sam, a missing name with a caller-selected

16
00:00:55.974 --> 00:00:58.450
fallback, and a count of three notes.

17
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:03.959
We’ll put each formatting rule in a small function and print the returned text

18
00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:04.925
to check it.

19
00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:09.375
Present text, missing text, and empty text are different cases.

20
00:01:09.625 --> 00:01:14.004
We’ll work through those differences before predicting the result of our

21
00:01:14.004 --> 00:01:14.800
sample calls.

22
00:01:15.902 --> 00:01:20.152
Let’s open demo.kts and build these formatting functions.

23
00:01:20.402 --> 00:01:25.579
The standard Run Code button will execute the script with the installed Kotlin

24
00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:25.977
compiler.

25
00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:30.479
First, several parts of the app could need the same display-name rule.

26
00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:35.705
We’ll give that rule one function, called displayName, so callers provide data

27
00:01:35.705 --> 00:01:37.779
instead of copying the decision.

28
00:01:45.737 --> 00:01:48.512
The fun keyword introduces the function.

29
00:01:48.762 --> 00:01:53.887
Its name parameter has a nullable String type, so it can hold text or null.

30
00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:56.412
Null means no value is present.

31
00:01:56.662 --> 00:02:01.937
The fallback parameter is ordinary non-null text, with Guest as its default.

32
00:02:02.187 --> 00:02:06.287
The return type promises that callers receive a non-null String.

33
00:02:06.537 --> 00:02:12.234
A default is used when the caller omits that argument; it does not replace

34
00:02:12.234 --> 00:02:13.862
every null argument automatically.

35
00:02:14.226 --> 00:02:18.809
Inside the function, we need to uppercase a name only when it exists, and

36
00:02:18.809 --> 00:02:21.101
still return text when it does not.

37
00:02:21.351 --> 00:02:26.395
A safe call lets us ask for the uppercase form without trying to call a method

38
00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:27.026
on null.

39
00:02:31.486 --> 00:02:36.522
When name contains text, the safe call runs uppercase and produces the

40
00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:37.361
converted text.

41
00:02:37.611 --> 00:02:41.186
When name is null, it skips that call and produces null.

42
00:02:41.436 --> 00:02:46.499
The Elvis operator then chooses fallback only if the value on its left is

43
00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:46.861
null.

44
00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:50.911
The return keyword sends the chosen text back to the caller.

45
00:02:51.162 --> 00:02:53.437
Work through a separate name, Riley.

46
00:02:53.687 --> 00:02:58.187
The safe call produces uppercase RILEY, so the fallback is unused.

47
00:02:58.437 --> 00:03:03.411
If the name is missing and the caller omits fallback, the safe call produces

48
00:03:03.411 --> 00:03:05.187
null and Guest is returned.

49
00:03:05.437 --> 00:03:10.766
An empty string is still a String, so its uppercase form stays empty and the

50
00:03:10.766 --> 00:03:12.187
fallback is not selected.

51
00:03:12.437 --> 00:03:15.687
Missing text and empty text need different rules.

52
00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:20.435
We could force a nullable value with a non-null assertion, often called double

53
00:03:20.435 --> 00:03:24.239
bang, but that would throw an error for a missing name.

54
00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:28.739
Our safe call and fallback express the behavior we want instead.

55
00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:34.426
We can reuse this function without asking every caller to repeat the same null

56
00:03:34.426 --> 00:03:34.814
handling.

57
00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:37.766
The header also needs a note-count label.

58
00:03:38.016 --> 00:03:43.131
That rule only builds one string, so we can write an expression-bodied

59
00:03:43.131 --> 00:03:46.541
function: its single expression supplies the returned value.

60
00:03:50.595 --> 00:03:52.820
The count parameter is an Int.

61
00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:58.020
Kotlin infers that the string-template expression returns a String, so this

62
00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.170
short form needs no explicit return keyword.

63
00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:05.070
With a separate count of four, the result would be four notes.

64
00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:10.497
This simple rule always uses the word notes; it does not handle singular

65
00:04:10.497 --> 00:04:10.895
grammar.

66
00:04:11.146 --> 00:04:13.071
Both functions are ready.

67
00:04:13.321 --> 00:04:17.471
Let’s add the three sample calls and print the values they return.

68
00:04:17.721 --> 00:04:22.885
Named arguments identify which parameter each value belongs to, making the

69
00:04:22.885 --> 00:04:26.171
missing-name test and its chosen fallback clear.

70
00:04:36.168 --> 00:04:40.118
The first call supplies Sam and leaves fallback at its default.

71
00:04:40.368 --> 00:04:45.118
The second explicitly supplies null for name and Visitor for fallback.

72
00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:49.493
The last supplies the integer three to the count-label function.

73
00:04:49.743 --> 00:04:52.843
Before we run, predict the second printed line.

74
00:04:53.093 --> 00:04:58.661
Trace the safe call first, then decide whether the Elvis operator uses its

75
00:04:58.661 --> 00:04:59.518
right-hand value.

76
00:04:59.768 --> 00:05:02.193
Why should that line match your prediction?

77
00:05:02.443 --> 00:05:04.643
Pause and explain your reasoning.

78
00:05:10.937 --> 00:05:15.187
Now run the script and compare the three actual lines with your trace.

79
00:05:24.672 --> 00:05:28.597
The first line is SAM in uppercase because its name exists.

80
00:05:28.847 --> 00:05:34.152
The second is Visitor: the safe call was skipped for null, and the explicitly

81
00:05:34.152 --> 00:05:36.047
supplied fallback became the result.

82
00:05:36.297 --> 00:05:39.472
The default Guest did not replace the caller’s choice.

83
00:05:39.722 --> 00:05:44.372
The final line is three notes, built by the count function’s string template.

84
00:05:44.624 --> 00:05:49.224
We’ve separated the header’s formatting rules from the places that call them.

85
00:05:49.474 --> 00:05:54.221
Function parameters carry the inputs, return values carry the results, and

86
00:05:54.221 --> 00:05:57.674
safe calls with a fallback handle absent names.

87
00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:02.874
The actual output confirms the present-name and missing-name paths in this

88
00:06:02.874 --> 00:06:03.774
small example.

89
00:06:04.031 --> 00:06:09.324
Now write a similar function for an optional note title with a caller-chosen

90
00:06:09.324 --> 00:06:09.731
fallback.

91
00:06:09.981 --> 00:06:13.231
Test a present title, null, and an empty string.

92
00:06:13.481 --> 00:06:18.203
Explain why the empty case differs from null, and decide whether your app

93
00:06:18.203 --> 00:06:19.656
should treat it differently.

94
00:06:19.906 --> 00:06:23.656
What returned values would convince you that each rule works?
